---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { modulePath } from '../lib/base';
import { formatDate, isoDate } from '../lib/format';
import { includeDrafts, publishedModules } from '../lib/collections';

const modules = publishedModules(await getCollection('modules'), {
  includeDrafts: includeDrafts(),
});
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Failure modes — the maths I only thought I understood"
  description="Each module takes one maths concept where I found my understanding was superficial: the belief itself, a case where it gives the wrong answer, and the single quantity that decides when it fails."
>
  <section class="intro prose">
    <h1>Measuring where mathematical intuition breaks</h1>
    <p>
      Working through STEP questions, I kept meeting the same thing: a concept I
      could use fluently, and a question built exactly where my understanding of it
      ran out. What was missing each time was not a step of the working. It was a
      belief about how the mathematics behaves — close enough to right to have
      survived every question I had met before, and wrong in the one place the
      question was standing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each entry is one of those beliefs. It names the belief, gives a concrete case
      where it produces a wrong answer, and identifies the single quantity that
      decides when it fails. They are meant to be read in any order, or one at a
      time when the belief in question is the one getting in your way.
    </p>
  </section>

  <ul class="module-list">
    {
      modules.map((entry) => (
        <li>
          <a href={modulePath(entry.id)}>
            <span class="claim">“{entry.data.claim}”</span>
            <span class="meta">
              <time datetime={isoDate(entry.data.added)}>{formatDate(entry.data.added)}</time>
            </span>
            <span class="context">
              {entry.data.title} · {entry.data.context}
            </span>
          </a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
</BaseLayout>
